rsed  in the language I spent an awful lot of  time listening and reading lots of other  people who lived in Japan  didn't take advantage in order for me to  be able to take advantage of that  environment that surrounded me I had to  bring my level up and that I did through  listening and reading of course I would  interact with people at an earlier stage  because I was right there so in a store  or wherever I had to try to use whatever  I had but those were meaningful  exchanges in the language I am not that  fond of having an artificial exchange  for the sake of having an exchange if  I'm where the language is spoken that's  a meaningful exchange then I get into it  and that becomes meaningful content so  none of that has changed what has  changed dramatically is the kind of  tools that are available so when I was  doing Chinese I had books I don't know  if it's worth showing I've shown them  before yeah you know I still have it  Chinese lectures at least 20 lectures in  Chinese culture don't go on fire show  John great simplified to some extent I  mean we had traditional characters that  you probably can't see that but there  were first of all we had the text in  pinyin we had like we had audio for it  and we had vocabulary lists per chapter  then I moved to this other excellent  book called intermediate reader in  modern Chinese which is just excellent  and there we're dealing with authentic  texts that was after three or four  months authentic text by Jo and live by  Mao Zedong bahu short and ample a  vocabulary list plus explanations not  explanations but it's pointing out the  structures you know I'm not gonna grab  but just one each on madama nehi schism  Adama or you know yo-yo ma whatever of  course in their explanations they were  full of v I looked at it you know the  ps1 diagrams this is a predicate this is  ever I never looked at any of that stuff  I just wanted to get a sense of the  structure this is the structure this is  how they say it and so that has always  been my approach but what has changed  now is and I'll quickly flat you know  not flash backwards but fast forward to  day from let's say 1974 1968-69  when I was studying Chinese in those  days I had this big open real tape  recorders alright and I look for books  that had vocabulary lists with each  chapter because looking things up in a  dictionary was so time-consuming and  hopeless today for example you know I I  happen to be watching television and  there's a Danish mu Danish movie called  a royal affair which is about the ruins  a period when there was a Mad King King  Christian in Denmark and it's a very  it's a lovely movie and I was trying  desperately to try and understand the  Danish but it was just too difficult  it's it's sufficiently different from  Sweden Swedish and it was just hard to  understand in the movie but it's a great  movie and I happen to have bought that  that's a Swedish novel and I happen to  have bought that novel in the 80s or  early 90s when I was working on my  Swedish and I haven't yet read it I  bought so many books that I don't read  sometimes so on up and had a look at it  and then I said wow that must exist in  eBook form so I then scoured the  Internet and was able to buy an e-book  the ebook comes in the ePub format which  I then put in the calibre this program  that I have that converts it into a text  format the text format then is a format  that I can import into link because my  vocabulary in Swedish because I was  there doing business and stuff for a lot  of stuff reading literature and so forth  there's a lot of missing words so I  would rather read this book in Swedish  on link where I can look up the words  and I have no trouble remembering them  because my Swedish is actually not that  bad even though I I'm missing some words  so it's not I say hey there must be an  audio book for this so I searched for  the audio book it turns out that audio  Pekka which I have already used for  Polish and for Czech and which has an  app that you install on your iPhone well  they have a Swedish version of audio  teka and lo and behold they have this  book which is called leave Lakeland's  beserk in Swedish so I download the  audio I now have it on my iPhone it's  not on my iPhone  it's being streamed but I can access it  by my iPhone so I can now access my  Russian history by a Coonan in Russia  from my iPhone I can access polish and  Czech resources Swedish resources from  my iPhone  meanwhile the texts I upload in the link  and I can read them I don't have to look  for vocabulary you know readers with  vocabulary notes or whatever so my  ability to study at any time wherever I  am to connect with polish Swedish you  know to read material where I can click  the lookup words that I don't know and  then I see them highlighted in yellow I  mean all of that has changed  dramatically but the basic principle  that it's going to be like stephen  krashen says it's going to be meaningful  content that will enable you to learn  the language you might trigger that  meaningful content by having a  conversation with someone meaningful  content for you might be music might be  German rock music that's not what  interests me but it has to be meaningful  content and if you are getting messages  you know words content that's meaningful  meaningful means you're interested and  you're able to understand it it might be  that you have to look up words in order  to understand it but again just  listening is something this noise is not  meaningful it has to be meaningful  interesting and yet you're kind of able  to understand it or can get where you  understand it that's what's gonna make  you learn the language once you accept  that then for example I'm doing some  Polish history and it's interesting I'm  enjoying reading about Polish history  I'm looking up words I'm not concerned  about my progress I mean yeah adlink we  record how many words are know and stuff  yeah Allah tells me as I'm busy I'm 